Negative control for scenario-player.spec.ts (T4.2, AC2.5/AC2.6 — design §3.1
ruling 2 / §7.1, the SCOPE-MISMATCH rule).

`world.boot` may only ever run at the scope the page is already showing: the
port it reaches is the page's OWN cached cell, so booting a different scope
takes T4.3's dispose branch — `destroy()` on the rendered surface — and leaves
the track driving an instance nobody can see. `arm` therefore compares the
track's declared scope with the page's and, on a mismatch, takes the page to
that scope path FIRST (a scope segment, so the page remounts per `P1-R2` and
mounts that cell itself), arming from `track=` on the other side.

This mutant removes that comparison, so a foreign-scope track arms in place and
its first scene boots a scope the page is not showing. The client-emails page's 11th
scenario ("Staff acting for a client read that client's collection") is exactly
this case: it declares a staff actor with a `for` context the bare page is not
on. Without this control that hole is unpinned — the bridge lane cannot see it
either (`tasks.md` K12: one world per fresh page load).

The mutant is deliberately surgical — `foreignScope` stays defined and unread,
which is the tell that the rule is the one thing that went.

Apply from the repo root: `git apply <this file>`; revert with `git apply -R`.

Reds: scenario-player.spec.ts — the scope-mismatch claims (a track whose
declared scope differs from the page's navigates to that scope path first and
boots nothing before it; the surface the track moves is the rendered one).

